-// Chapter 29 checkpoint 3: march the segment, refine the crossing, bound the thickness, and finish the miss taxonomy.
+// Chapter 29 checkpoint 4: decomposed confidence and one application of the split-sum weight.
 //
-// The traversal introduced in the previous checkpoint now walks the segment it measured. The step schedule is
-// uniform in screen space, every sample interpolates UV and 1/viewDepth in the same parameter, and the first step
-// that passes behind the sampled surface is refined by bisection. A depth buffer stores one surface and not a solid,
-// so a crossing only counts as contact when the ray is behind that surface by no more than a finite thickness
-// interval; everything deeper is information the screen does not have. The outcomes that only marching can decide -
-// thickness exceeded, no crossing, the exhausted step budget, and the exhausted ray length - complete the typed miss
-// taxonomy, and the step, refinement, geometry-sample, and thickness-rejection counts are published so the cost of
-// the search is visible. The shaded colour is still the environment-only baseline: this checkpoint decides what the
-// screen contains without spending it. A hierarchical depth pyramid would let the same ray skip empty screen
-// regions; that acceleration is a separate contract and is deliberately not running here.
+// A screen-space hit is evidence, not certainty. The classified trace becomes six independently bounded confidence
+// factors - validity, screen-edge proximity, distance travelled, thickness agreement, roughness, and how far the ray
+// points back toward the camera - and they stay decomposed so a learner can see which assumption failed. Their
+// product is a fade weight, not a probability. Screen radiance and environment radiance are then combined with the
+// weights confidence and 1 - confidence, which sum to exactly one, so the incoming radiance is a partition of a
+// single quantity instead of a sum of two guesses. The split-sum weight F0 * A + B is applied once, after that
+// combination, so a partial screen answer can never be shaded twice.
